{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure","id":1514702,"title":"V5 Tzimisce - clan structure","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-06-12T08:13:25-07:00","id":1514702,"title":"V5 Tzimisce - clan structure","content":"I was working on a new character for fun, doing a bit of research here and there, coming up with a cool concept and all. I was surprised V5 has no information on the Tzimisce, nothing. I know they were kinda of an after thought, being introduced in the companion alongside the Ravnos and Salubri. <br /> <br /> The Tzimisce were the Sabbat, with the Sabbat gone they had to change the core of this clan changing them to focus more on the dragon, hoarding and all. Which is fine, I guess. This write up begs for a new view on the clan, or at lest a way to evolve into something new.<br /> <br /> V5 does not lean into clans a whole lot, with exeption of the Hecata \u2014 which is not that deep\u2014 but there something missing for me, I want to know how the fiends of today see themselves, are they still a clan? The old clan rose to power? What are your opinions on the state of the clan Tzimisce?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27426-nometao","name":"Nometao","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-12T08:23:00-07:00","id":1514704,"title":"","content":"I'd just ignore the characterization of V5 and just default to revised power structure with membership of the Sabbat considered the personal discretion of individual dragons/fiends. It's probably worth noting you've already put more thought into it than the authors did.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 06-12-2023, 11:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-12T08:48:18-07:00","id":1514707,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nometao</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure#post1514702\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Tzimisce were the Sabbat</div> </div> </div> </div>Not entirely<br /> The Old Clan and the Koldun were basically independent<br /> The Tzimisce were never widely political creatures to begin with; merely territorial, and their choice of allegiance reflected this<br /> The Oradea League is an alliance of a dozen Old Clan Tzimisce elders that refused to bow down before the Sabbat. They maintain their own feudal lands around the Romanian city of Oradea, much like they did during the Dark Ages. The League was \u2013 and still is \u2013 based on a set of absolute rules, designed exclusively to protect the unlives of the founders<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nometao</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure#post1514702\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">V5 does not lean into clans a whole lot, with exeption of the Hecata \u2014 which is not that deep\u2014 but there something missing for me, I want to know how the fiends of today see themselves, are they still a clan? The old clan rose to power? What are your opinions on the state of the clan Tzimisce?</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> <br /> The Fifth edition <b>Grounded</b>\u200b Bane and <b>Covetousness</b>\u200b Compulsion I think are a good pointer how they see themselves<br /> Two quotes about the Old Clan nicely reflect in my humble opinion how a V5 Tzimisice could see itself:<br /> <br /> &quot;Thousands of human lives have been taken by my hands. I am a protector of humanity, but my vigilance comes with a price.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;<i>Old ways do not die. Like old Cainites, they</i> merely sleep, awaiting sufficient blood to reawaken them.\u200b&quot;<br /> <br /> I could easly see the old tradition or Laws of <i>Hospitality </i>gaining prominence again<br /> The rights and treatment of guests forms an indelible part of Tzimisce culture. There are many archaic rules concerning deportment, manners, greetings, goodbyes, allowances, and settlement of grievances, but there are a few Clan-wide obligations that are always honored. A guest of the Tzimisce is entitled to several things:<ul><li>Shelter and nourishment for three days and three nights (not counting the night of arrival)</li> <li>The protection of the host against third-party aggressors</li> <li>Suspension of inter-family grievances for the duration of the stay</li> <li>The best quarters in the home of the host (up to and including the host's own chambers)</li> </ul>However, the guest also has a few obligations that makes them worthy of such special treatment<ul><li>A ritual exchange of gifts or services (on arriving, departing, or sometimes both)</li> <li>Respecting the host's boundaries and property</li> <li>Not needlessly angering the host or any of his family</li> <li>Not staying any longer than the requisite three days and three nights unless invited to.</li> </ul>The balance of these laws is to ensure that no advantage is taken of either the host or guest \u2013 a Fiend's honor can be completely shattered by infringing upon either one of these sets of rules. Given the warfare that exists between lineages of Tzimisce, exercising patience and honoring these laws is a way to ensure that there is always a sort of &quot;neutral ground&quot; where combative families can meet and prevent any further outrages.<br /> <br /> The older a Tzimisce is, the more seriously they treat these laws, for they remember the nights when the only shelter to be had was in the domain of a rival voivode, when travel was arduous and only undertaken when necessary. Most elder Tzimisce respect these laws in the modern nights, but their grandchilder may not even be aware that these laws exist, much less still enforced in their ancestors' domains. Minor breaches can be forgiven, though some effort must be made to show that the trespasser is apologetic.<br /> Major breaches can fix a Tzimisce's reputation in a very bad way \u2013 slaughtering guests during the day is a sure way to make sure no one comes to visit you, or ever wants to make a deal.<br /> <br /> Even the Embrace could reflect this, with the old Koldunic ritual, <b>Ritual of Death's Embrace</b>:\u200b<br /> \u200b<br /> \u200bThis cruel ritual permits a vampire to extend the process of the Embrace over many nights, offering the pleasure of repeated feeding even as the victim sinks into death and unlife.<br /> <b>System:</b> The koldun drinks a goblet containing a foul mixture of wine, ashes and his vitae. If the ritual succeeds, the vampire may begin the process. Each night, he drains one to three points of human blood, replacing it with a like quantity of his own vitae. By the power of the ritual, this blood does not convert the mortal into a ghoul; rather, it slowly kills her as from deepining anemia. Her skin grows wain and pallid, her breathing shallows, and her heartbeat slows. The most skilled healers will assume she suffers a disease of the blood. Though no remedy can truly halt the transformation, bloodletting does slow it. The mortal bleeds one point of human blood followed by one point of vitae, alternating until all the vitae is gone or she dies from blood loss. Once the koldun transfers 10 points of vitae, replacing all the mortal's blood, the victim undergoes the actual Embrace. She dies and arises upon the next sunset as a Cainite. Only cremation or dismemberment may prevent this rise. During the entire ritual, the death of the koldun restores the victim to full health within a day. By its very nature, this ritual also confers the blood oath upon the victim.<br /> <br /> Accordingly, Tzimisce society is structured around individual broods comprising a sire and one or more Blood Bound childer. Childer fill the roles of lovers, family, friends, bodyguards, and servants. Tzimisce mastery over the Blood Bond allows the sire to attune the emotions of his childer to the desired pitch. Thus, a vampire lover may be Blood Bound to feel all-consuming desire for the sire, a guard may be Blood Bound for loyalty, and a mate may be &quot;programmed&quot; for love. The fact that these emotions are artificial and one-sided rarely bothers the sire.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-12T19:22:17-07:00","id":1514787,"title":"","content":"Clans don\u2019t bring that much structure, as a rule. That\u2019s where sects come in. And not all members of a clan are going to belong to a given sect, so you can\u2019t default to the sect in order to describe clan culture. That\u2019s a mistake previous editions made, when the Tzimisce ended up being Clan Sabbat and Clan Vicissitude instead of having a culture outside of those things. It\u2019s not until the Dark Ages stuff that they got a proper presentation.<br /> <br /> As for the Sabbat, they still exist, as there are many Tzimisce in the sect, but the sect culture downplays clan identity because it derives from the Antediluvians. Tzimisce among the Camarilla and Anarchs will probably place more emphasis on tradition and lineage. They're more common among the Anarchs simply because they prefer to be laws unto themselves rather than bending the knee to others, but it's up to the indidivual and the domain.<br /> <br /> By nature they are territorial and seek to control what they have, so you can expect traditionalist Tzimisce childer to be bound and kept close, and for there to be a lot of deference for elders and respect for territory. Values that fit in well with the Camarilla. But of course that\u2019s the sort of thing that made so many of them rebel and turn Anarch in the first place. And modern Tzimisce Anarchs, well, could be like anything at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":352,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","user":"Black Flag","date":". 06-30-2023, 03:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-13T01:07:37-07:00","id":1514808,"title":"","content":"Most V5 Tzimisce are Sabbat or Anarchs. Stronger clan structure would be found among the minorities of bloodlines with no Sabbat or Anarch heritage, like in the Oradea League or the Camarilla. Semi-signature character Mayumi Shibasaki alluded to her place in the local clan structure, but I heard she got wiped in the SI book, before we learnt anything substantial.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3523-spencer-from-the-hills","name":"Spencer from The Hills","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"546"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T02:32:51-07:00","id":1514929,"title":"","content":"Lineage is also important to the Tzimisce<br /> Maybe an official family tree also helps<br /> <br /> <b>The Eldest </b>(3d Generation)<br /> Sire of Kartarirya<br /> Sire of Gallod<br /> Sire of Demdemeh<br /> Sire of Byelobog<br /> Sire of Ionache<br /> Sire of Djavakhi<br /> Sire of the Dracon<br /> Sire of Tabak<br /> Sire of Lord Ruthven<br /> Sire of Triglav<br /> Sire of Yorak<br /> Sire of Cyscek<br /> Sire of Andeleon<br /> Sire of Toomler<br /> The Legend of Irad has it that, at some point in the city of Enoch, Irad the Strong sought to purge himself of all evil. Using his mastery of Protean, the ancient gathered all of his evil into a bilious mass of repugnancy and horror, and spat it out. Irad's intention was to destroy the mortal who consumed this mass of Protean, thinking that by destroying this mortal, he would destroy the evil also. But to Irad's surprise, this mortal appeared normal. Irad brought the mortal to his brother attention, who - out of Curiousity - Embraced the mortal to see what the outcome would be.<br /> According to texts in the Library of the Forgotten, Ynosh the Lawgiver was attracted to the insights of the man that he would Embrace<br /> Thus, the world was given the Tzimisce Antediluvian, whos sire is both Enoch and Irad in a sense<br /> [Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised, DAV: Players Guide to High Clans]<br /> <br /> <b>Kartarirya</b>, the Many-Armed, the first childe of the Tzimisce Antediluvian. (4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> Sire of Ratti-Ben<br /> Considers all of India to be its personal hunting grounds; dwells in his haven on the isle of Sri Lanka<br /> [Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Ratti-Ben</b>, the Sister of Blood, the eldest childe of Kartarirya (5th Generation)<br /> She assumed leadership over its brood after Kartarirya fell into torpor. Her haven is in an abandoned temple in Vrindavan and she employs dozens of old crones to sing her name during the day.<br /> [Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Gallod</b> (4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> Was a tribal chieftain that lived in the Fertile Crescent. He was the second progeny of the Tzimisce Antediluvian, Embraced to keep an eye on the events of the First City. However, when the Eldest frenzied out of hunger, Gallod was consumed by his sire from afar, devoured from inside out. After the accident, the Antediluvian began traveling with a mortal entourage, to ensure a ready supply of vitae.<br /> No known progenies<br /> [Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Demdemeh </b>(4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> Sire of Sycorax<br /> The first African who encountered the Eldest.<br /> Built the city of Khamissa in the primordial jungle that would one day become the Sahara, battled the Mokol\u00e9 and he carved out an realm for himself where he ruled over the jungles with an iron fist until finally fell into torpor.<br /> It was only when his childe Sycorax and her get were in grave dangers by the wizards of the Yor\u00f9b\u00e1, that he arose anew, but even he could not turn the tide in this conflict and fell finally into torpor in a hidden valley west of Lake Victoria.<br /> It is thought by various Tzimisce researchers that Demdemeh has transformed himself into a viral form, partitioning himself into various strings and infecting other viruses like Ebola in order to spread his essence. It is said that much of the African wildlife is infected with him and a group of researchers of the University of Marburg brought the virus 1967 into Europe, although greater consequences have not yet been seen. It is said that various &quot;elephants' graveyards&quot; are actually parts of Demdemeh that strive to reunite with the whole.<br /> [Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Sycorax</b>, the Witch (5th Generation)<br /> Children of Demdemeh<br /> Sire of (allegedly) Landulf II of Capua<br /> [Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Landulf II of Capua</b> (6th Generation)<br /> Children of (allegedly) Sycorax<br /> Named the third most important man in the kingdom of Emperor Louis II, he controlled land from Naples to Calabria until the Vatican allegedly excommunicated him in 875. Rather than suffer under Carpathian influence, Landulf allowed an African Fiend, (possibly Sycorax) who claimed lineage from the methuselah Demdemeh, to Embrace him.<br /> In the Modern Nights, Landulf is one of the few bridges between the western Tzimisce and their African brethren.<br /> [Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Byelobog</b>, the White God (4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> Ruled the lands between the Baltic Sea, close to the Pinsk Marshes of Lithuania. He was revered and feared as the &quot;White God&quot; by the inhabitants.<br /> He was a mighty koldun with power over the waters within his domain \u2013 having mastery over the swamp-things, the fogs and, above all, the howling snows that grip the land in winter. He created a revenant line called Khazi<br /> Byelobog was diablerized by Lugoj Blood-breaker (It is unknown whether or not Lugoj had already been substituted by the Tzimisce Antediluvian by the time he diablerized Byelobog. Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised hints that the Eldest was disappointed with some of its most powerful childer and both Yorak and Byelobog were destroyed under suspicious circumstances)<br /> No known progenies<br /> [Libellus Sanguinis 1: Masters of the State, Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Ionache</b> (4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> It was he who created the pseudo-feudal system of the Voivodate, and named himself the first Voivode. His territories lay in Western Hungary and he ruled over them with an iron fist, allowing no intrusions into his domain.<br /> No known progenies<br /> [Players Guide to High Clans]<br /> <br /> <b>Djavakhi </b>(4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> Sire of Noriz<br /> [Dark Ages Clan Novel 13: Tzimisce]<br /> <br /> <b>Noriz</b>, the Corrupter of Legions (5th Generation)<br /> Children of Djavakhi<br /> Sire of Koban<br /> Sire of Lukasz Brancoveanu<br /> Sire of Rachlav<br /> Sire of Lugoj<br /> Ruler of Moldavia. Voivode among Voivodes. During the Anarch Revolt, he was eventually killed by his childe, Koban.<br /> [Transylvania By Night, Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised, Dark Ages Clan Novel 13: Tzimisce, ]<br /> <br /> <b>Koban</b>, Fiend Among Fiends (6th Generation)<br /> Koldun. During the Anarch Revolt, he was eventually killed by one his children<br /> Children of Noriz<br /> Sire of Dominik<br /> [Bitter Crusade]<br /> <br /> <b>Dominik </b>(7th Generation)<br /> Children of Koban<br /> [Bitter Crusade]<br /> <br /> <b>Lukasz Brancoveanu</b> (6th Generation)<br /> Children of Noriz<br /> Sire of Ioan Brancoveanu cel Macelar<br /> [Dark Ages Clan Novel 13: Tzimisce]<br /> <br /> <b>Voivode Ioan Brancoveanu cel Macelar</b>, the Hammer of the Tremere, the Butcher (7th Generation)<br /> His lands lie in the mountains south-east of Karlsburg and south-west of Hermanstadt. Although ruthless, this childe of Lukasz was deemed an honorable Tzimisce who abided by his clan's tradition. Ioan himself was a koldun, a master of the ways of the air.<br /> Children Lukasz Brancoveanu<br /> [Dark Ages Clan Novel 13: Tzimisce]<br /> <br /> <b>Rachlav</b>, the Unquenchable (6th Generation)<br /> He has been at war with Lukasz Brancoveanu since before 1100<br /> [Dark Ages Clan Novel 13: Tzimisce]<br /> <br /> <b>Lugoj</b>, the Blood-Breaker (6th-5th-4th Generation after consuming Byelobog)<br /> Children of Noriz<br /> Sire of Baian<br /> Died in 1413 in the Sernog Monastery where the Tzimisce Antediluvian took the form of Lugoj and proceeded to fake his own diablerie, successfully fooling the Anarchs into thinking that he was actually dead and that the real Lugoj had prevailed.<br /> [Transylvania By Night, Libellus Sanguinis 1: Masters of the State, Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Baian </b>(5th Generation)<br /> Children of Lugoj<br /> Sire Szechenyi Jol\u00e1n<br /> [Mexico City by Night]<br /> <br /> <b>Szechenyi Jol\u00e1n</b>, the Mother of Horrors (6th Generation)<br /> Children Baian<br /> Vlaszy revenant who was Embraced into the Tzimisce clan. She was one of the most knowledgeable experts in Tzimisce history and traditions. Helped to develop a purely discipline-based method of creating a Vozhd<br /> [Mexico City by Night]<br /> <br /> <b>The Dracon</b> of Cyprus(4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> Sire of Nikosya<br /> Sire of the Keeper of the Faith<br /> Sire of Zdravka<br /> Sire of Gesu<br /> Unlike many of his childer, the Eldest offered him a true choice when the time came: the gift of his blood or the right to live and die as a mortal, a life in which he would not interfere. It is said that the Antediluvian loved him, not as a man loves his lover, but as a father loves his son.<br /> He created the Obertus Family revenant line<br /> [Constantinople by Night, Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised, Dark Ages Clan Novel 13: Tzimisce]<br /> <br /> <b>Nikosya</b> (5th Generation)<br /> Children of the Dracon<br /> [V20 Dark Ages Tome of Secrets]<br /> <br /> <b>Karpeslav</b> (6th Generation)<br /> Children of Nikosya<br /> Sire of Pyotr Stanislav<br /> [V20 Dark Ages Tome of Secrets]<br /> <br /> Holy Father <b>Pyotr Stanislav</b>, Most Holy Koldun of the Tchernokrazym Flayer of Sins (7th Generation)<br /> Children of Karpeslav<br /> [V20 Dark Ages Tome of Secrets]<br /> <br /> <b>Zdravka</b> (5th Generation)<br /> Children of the Dracon<br /> Sire of Nikita of Sredetz<br /> [Dark Ages Clan Novel 13: Tzimisce]<br /> <br /> <b>Nikita</b> of Sredetz (6th Generation)<br /> He was an obsessive collector of potentially historically significant minutiae and never threw anything away, no matter how ancient, virtually illegible, or patently insane it might be.<br /> Nikita was anointed as the new Archbishop of Nod in 1212. He was eventually destroyed by his grandsire, who assumed his shape in order to destroy the Cainite Heresy for their involvement in the Bitter Crusade.<br /> [Dark Ages Clan Novel 13: Tzimisce]<br /> <br /> <b>The Keeper of the Faith</b>, Guardian of the Library of the Forgotten (5th Generation)<br /> Children of the Dracon<br /> Sire of Alexus Simocatta<br /> Sire of the Watchers<br /> [Constantinople by Night]<br /> <br /> <b>Alexus Simocatta</b> (6th Generation)<br /> Children of the Keeper of the Faith<br /> He was assassinated by an unknown Assamite in 1481.<br /> [Jerusalem by Night]<br /> <br /> <b>The Watchers</b> (6th Generation)<br /> Childrens of the Keeper of the Faith<br /> The Keeper of the Faith was the guardian of the Library of the Forgotten, in her duties she was accompanied by two childer, known only as &quot;the watchers&quot;.<br /> [Constantinople by Night]<br /> <br /> <b>Gesu</b>, Saint of the Divinity Within (5th Generation)<br /> Children of the Dracon<br /> Sire of Symeon<br /> Sire of Zubor (Obertus)<br /> Sire of Lillian<br /> [Constantinople by Night, Bitter Crusade]<br /> <br /> <b>Symeon</b>, His Brother's Keeper (6th Generation)<br /> Children of Gesu<br /> Sire of Myca Vykos<br /> He held court in the city of Oradea and was involved in peace negotiations to unify his Tzimisce brethren when, in 1550, was utterly betrayed and diablerised by his childe Vykos<br /> [Constantinople by Night]<br /> <br /> <b>Myca/Sascha Vykos</b> (7th Generation-6th via Diableire)<br /> Children of Symeon<br /> [Constantinople by Night]<br /> <br /> <b>Zubor </b>(Obertus) (6th Generation)<br /> Children of Gesu<br /> Sire of Bodor Toth<br /> [Bitter Crusade]<br /> <br /> <b>Bodor Toth</b>, Father of the Brood<br /> Cildren of Zubor (7th Generation)<br /> Sire of Farkas Toth<br /> Sire of Erzebet Toth<br /> Bodor fell at the hands of his mortal son Kili\u00e1n in 1238<br /> [Bitter Crusade]<br /> <br /> <b>Farkas Toth</b> (8th Generation)<br /> Children of Bodor Toth<br /> Died around or during the Fourth Crusade<br /> [Bitter Crusade]<br /> <br /> <b>Erzebet Toth</b> (8th Generation)<br /> Children of Bodor Toth<br /> [Bitter Crusade]<br /> <br /> <b>Lillian</b> (6th Generation)<br /> Children of Symeon<br /> In life, she was the mortal lover of Symeon. She was Embraced by Gesu in 1196, and he melded her into his chest. He believed that in doing so he had created the holy perfect body of the human.<br /> She presumably suffered Final Death with Gesu when he was burned to death in 1204.<br /> [Constantinople by Night]<br /> <br /> <b>Tabak</b> (4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> Sire of Lambach Ruthven<br /> Vlad &quot;persuaded&quot; Lambach to turn him into a vampire. Immediately thereafter, he diablerized the defenseless Tabak in 1495<br /> [Transylvania Chronicles II: Son of the Dragon, Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Lambach Ruthven</b> (5th Generation)<br /> Children of Tabak<br /> Sire of Count Dracula<br /> Sire of Caltuna<br /> [Transylvania Chronicles II: Son of the Dragon, Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> Count Vlad III Dr\u0103culea &quot;\u021aepe\u0219&quot;, <b>Count Dracula</b> the Impaler, Son of The Dragon (6th Generation-5th via Diableire)<br /> Children Lambach Ruthven<br /> Sire of Lucy Westernra<br /> Sire of Wilhelmina Harker<br /> Member of the Basarabs Ravenant line's Draculesti branch, Koldun and Inconnu<br /> Vlad &quot;persuaded&quot; Lambach to turn him into a vampire. Immediately thereafter, he diablerized the defenseless Tabak.<br /> [Transylvania Chronicles II: Son of the Dragon, Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Lucy Westernra</b> (6th Generation)<br /> Children of Count Dracula<br /> She was slain shortly after her Embrace (provided that the Bram Stoker's novel was true)<br /> [Bram Stoker's Dracula]<br /> <br /> <b>Wilhelmina Harker</b> (6th Generation)<br /> Children of Count Dracula<br /> Sire of Alex<br /> Noddist Scholar<br /> [Vampire: the Masquerade 1st and 2nd editions, VTM: 5th Edition Corebook]<br /> <br /> <b>Alex </b>(7th Generation)<br /> Children Wilhelmina Harker<br /> [VTM: 5th Edition Corebook]<br /> <br /> <b>Caltuna</b> (6th Generation)<br /> Children of Lambach Ruthven<br /> [Beckett's Jyhad Diary]<br /> <br /> <b>Lord Ruthven </b>(4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> Sire of Damek Ruthven<br /> Creator of the Ruthvenski Revenantline. The ancient Revenant family in the service of the Ruthven line of the Tzimisce clan. In modern nights, they were absorbed into surviving families through intermarriage.<br /> [Dark Ages Clan Novel 13: Tzimisce, Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Damek Ruthven</b>, Voivode of Sarmizegetusa (5th Generation)<br /> Children of Lord Ruthven<br /> Sire of Danika Ruthven<br /> He is one of the most renowned genealogists and scholars of the history of clan Tzimisce. He was also a skilled koldun in the Spirit Ways and a noted Tzimisce warlord in his own right.<br /> Secretly the protector of the Tzimisce Antediluvian God-Tree form<br /> [Dark Ages Clan Novel 13: Tzimisce, Lair of the Hidden]<br /> <br /> <b>Danika Ruthven</b>, Mistress of Hunedoara (6th Generation)<br /> Children of Damek Ruthven<br /> Sire of Anastazi Ruthven<br /> Mighty Tzimisce koldun, member of the Council of Twelve, the ruling body of the Inconnu.<br /> [Dark Ages Clan Novel 13: Tzimisce, Lair of the Hidden]<br /> <br /> <b>Anastazi Ruthven</b> (7th Generation-6th via Diableire)<br /> Children of Danika Ruthven<br /> [Lair of the Hidden]<br /> <br /> <b>Triglav</b>, the Three-Headed (4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> Sire of Shaagra<br /> Sire of Velya<br /> Was one of the most powerful Tzimisce within the Old Country, although he answered to the authority of Yorak. He was a renowned koldun prophet.<br /> The Dracon tore Triglav's three heads from his body, destroying him in 702 CE<br /> [Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Shaagra </b>(5th Generation)<br /> Children of Triglav<br /> She helped Libussa, her Ghoul, settle their tribe in Bohemia, choosing as her haven the location where Prague would one day rise. Shaagra fell into torpor, haunted by feverish dreams about Kupala and served by her descendants<br /> She decided to support Vladimir Rustovich from her base in Prague by undermining the Ventrue war efforts within the Holy Roman Empire itself and eventually ousting the Ventrue themselves from their domain.<br /> Shaagra slipped into torpor and only awakened sporadically, and finally met her end during World War II when her haven was hit by a bomb and she burned to ashes.<br /> [Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised, Transylvania by Night]<br /> <br /> <b>Velya</b> the Flayer, the Vivisectionist, Cardinal of the Land Beyond the Forest (5th Generation)<br /> Children of Triglav<br /> Sire of Elaine Cassidy<br /> He and Myca Vykos were the chief conspirators of Lugoj Blood-Breaker in the formative years of the Anarch Revolt. Together they broke their blood bonds using Kupala's sacred fire-flower<br /> Founding member of the Sabbat and one of - if not the - Sabbat's mightiest koldun<br /> [Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Elaine Cassidy </b>(6th Generation)<br /> Children of Velya<br /> The horrors of the Sabbat were too much for her childish intellect to cope with, and Elaine was lost to the Beast and had become a Wight. In desperation, her Sire used Vicissitude to fuse her body with his, in an attempt to &quot;fix&quot; her. But no Cainite, even as one as clever as Velya, can rescue another from the throes of the Beast.<br /> [Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised]<br /> <br /> <b>Yorak</b>, Voivode among Voivodes, High Priest of Clan Tzimisce, Master (or Mistress depending of Yorak's mood) of the Cathedral of Flesh (4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> Sire of Kosczecsyku<br /> Was a devoted koldun and Metamorphosist, sought to further the clan's understanding of the transcendent state of Azhi Dahaka.<br /> Yorak's mastery of the flesh-crafting arts is almost without peer. It is said that he can appear as anything or anyone at a moment's notice, from a fly on the wall to a colossal monstrosity plucked from the deepest, darkest terrors of the racial memories of humanity. However, when treating with visitors or aspirants to the Cult of Metamorphosis he often chooses to appear as a feeble, wizened, wicked old man.<br /> In 1494 the Cathedral of Flesh turned against Yorak possibly thanks to the direct influence of Kupala (or the Eldest) itself \u2013 and committed diablerie on its keeper.<br /> [Clanbook: Tzimisce Revised, Transylvania by Night]<br /> <br /> <b>Kosczecsyku </b>(5th Generation)<br /> Children of Yorak<br /> Sire of Vrisa<br /> Sire of Mischa<br /> Sire of Csiskos<br /> Sire of Vladimir Rustovich<br /> Faked his death in 983 CE. Mighty Koldun. Even in modern nights he is the proud owner of a vozhd.<br /> [Transylvania by Night, WTA: Rage: Warriors of the Apocalypse, Rites of the Blood]<br /> <br /> <b>Vrisa</b> (6th Generation)<br /> Children of Kosczecsyku<br /> Favored childe and apprentice of Kosczecsyku.<br /> She is described as a perfect porcelain manikin with white skin and black hair, wearing immaculate white dresses with soft gloves and black patent leather shoes. A velvet bow pulled the curls away from her cheekbones, lending the girl's eyes a strange, sculpted look.<br /> [Rites of the Blood]<br /> <br /> <b>Mischa </b>(6th Generation)<br /> Children of Kosczecsyku<br /> Destroyed in 1313<br /> [Transylvania by Night, WTA: Rage: Warriors of the Apocalypse]<br /> <br /> <b>Csiskos</b> (6th Generation)<br /> Children of Kosczecsyku<br /> Destroyed in 1313<br /> [Transylvania by Night, WTA: Rage: Warriors of the Apocalypse]<br /> <br /> <b>Count Vladimir Rustovich</b>, Voivode among Voivodes (6th Generation)<br /> Children of Kosczecsyku<br /> Sire of Roland<br /> Sire of Osobei Valisevich<br /> Sire of Kara Vlaszy Lupescu<br /> Formerly a Bratovitch revenant, Vladimir assumed both the mantle of Cainite, the title of Voivode, and his sire's estate through diligence, might, and a modicum of treachery. He became renown even during his mortal days fighting numerous battles against the many enemies of his clan, and after his sire fell in battle against werewolves, Vladimir led the campaign against both his rivals and the culprit Garou. He proved himself a ferocious warrior and cunning strategists in the many wars against the Shadow Lords, the invading Ventrue, anarchistic Gangrel, the upstart Tremere and their Gargoyle slaves, and rival Tzimisce. While he battled Teutonic knights in 1313, his jealous brood mates plotted against him and arranged a ambush on his castle by the Shadow Lords, who killed all his servitors and brides. When Vladimir returned his Frenzy was terrible. Stories of the massacre are told even today on the campfires of Shadow Lord moots and even Tzimisce shudder when they hear what he did to his brood mates during the terrors of the Anarch Revolt, whom he joined out of spite and bitterness.<br /> In Modern Nights, Vladimir's main concern was his revenge against the hated Lupines. Aware of Past Lives, he hunted the descendants of those Shadow Lords that attacked his castle. He claimed membership in the Sabbat, but did not concern himself with their goals, using them as a fa\u00e7ade for his revenge. Around 2002, Rustovich murdered the father of Evelyn Constantine (Shadow Lord). In vengeance, Constantine and her sept called in several favors and Rustovich was killed<br /> [Transylvania by Night, WTA: Rage: Warriors of the Apocalypse]<br /> <br /> <b>Roland </b>(7th Generation)<br /> Children of Vladimir Rustovich<br /> Died around 1022 after giving the Embrace to two Tremere apprenticesof Goratrix, Stephan and Pharus<br /> Sire of Stephan<br /> Sire of Pharus<br /> [House of Tremere]<br /> <br /> <b>Stephan</b> (8th Generation)<br /> Children of Roland<br /> Goratrix's Ritual of Usurpation involved the sacrifice and consumption of two Tremere apprentices to Goratrix, Stephan and Pharus, both of whom had been embraced by the Tzimisce Elder Roland at the behest of Goratrix just prior to Roland's destruction by the Archmage<br /> [House of Tremere]<br /> <br /> <b>Pharus </b>(8th Generation)<br /> Children of Roland<br /> Goratrix's Ritual of Usurpation involved the sacrifice and consumption of two Tremere apprentices to Goratrix, Stephan and Pharus, both of whom had been embraced by the Tzimisce Elder Roland at the behest of Goratrix just prior to Roland's destruction by the Archmage<br /> [House of Tremere]<br /> <br /> <b>Osobei Valisevich </b>(7th Generation)<br /> Children of Vladimir Rustovich<br /> [Dark Ages Clan Novel 11: Tremere]<br /> <br /> <b>Kara Vlaszy Lupescu</b>, Fiendish Knight (7th Generation)<br /> Children of Vladimir Rustovich<br /> During the Anarch Revolt, Kara joined the Sabbat alongside her sire. An unspecified amount of time later, however, she was killed by Lupines in the woods of Wallachia.<br /> [Under the Black Cross]<br /> <br /> <b>Cyscek </b>(4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> Rosenkratz the Younger (Childe of Rosenkratz the Elder, Chile of Hardestadt the Elder, Childe of Ueruic/Erik Eigermann, Childe of Veddartha) refers to him as &quot;one of his dearest fellows&quot;<br /> No known progenies<br /> [VTM: Gehenna]<br /> <br /> <b>Andeleon </b>(4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> Originally was a pharmakos in Ancient Greece, who practiced human sacrifice to power his magic. The Eldest, envious of this art, Embraced Andeleon and made him his emissary to the protectors of Enoch.<br /> [The Black Hand: Guide to the Tal'Mahe'Ra, Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand]<br /> <br /> <b>Toomler </b>(4th Generation)<br /> Children of the Eldest<br /> He allegedly possessed very unusual powers, including the ability to disassemble parts of his body and then reconnect them.<br /> Toomler perished under unknown circumstances, but some pieces of his body still remained. His femur is a vampiric artifact rumored to cause horrible wounds even when used against another Cainite. It was supposedly used after Toomler\u2019s demise by another Tzimisce to kill one of the founders of the Tremere clan.<br /> [Players Guide to the Sabbat 1992 edtion]<br /> <br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4},{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-16T00:34:16-07:00","id":1515215,"title":"","content":"Clans really don\u2019t seem to be a priority for Paradox, what with the laughable claim that predator types are more important, and the fact that the moment they had a chance to make an NPC vampire faction they jumped straight to \u201cyeah they don\u2019t have Clans, they just have Enemy Classes- I mean Paths\u201d. <br /> <br /> I doubt we\u2019ll get more info than what\u2019s already here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27967-caerolion","name":"Caerolion","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-19T13:32:37-07:00","id":1515556,"title":"","content":"Given the 5th edition metaplot updates, it's only sensible for the Tzimisce to have been all but destroyed. The majority of its methuselahs, elders, and ancillae have either been Beckoned, destroyed (regardless of by whom), or fucked off to participate in the Gehenna crusade. The gross excesses of the revenant families are too much to hide in the face of the SI to say nothing of the cartoonish villainy of metamorphosist Tzimisce. The clan's dalliance with sectarian politics ended exactly where it always would: the original Anarchs' work destroying clan identity and culture for a shared, sectarian, identity in the Sabbat meant the clan would all but die with it.<br /> <br /> Who's left? Tzimisce that didn't chug the Sabbat Kool-aid, young enough to avoid the Beckoning, and subtle enough to avoid the Second Inquisition. So...not very bloody many. It only makes sense they'd be &quot;back to square one&quot;: isolated, local domains ruled by broods of sires and their childer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-19T14:16:35-07:00","id":1515565,"title":"","content":"I don't read anything about it, the information I have is from third parties, so I'm sorry if there are any inconsistencies, but what I've heard is that both the Tzimice clan and a good part of Ministry migrated to the anarch movement, elders were called and the younger ones, from the 11th generation onwards, joined for lack of options.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T07:13:04-07:00","id":1516643,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1515556#post1515556\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Who's left? Tzimisce that didn't chug the Sabbat Kool-aid,</div> </div> </div> </div>why wouldnt they drink the kool-aid? From a tzmisce perspective the sabbat was/is right about everything. If anything as the only cohesive clan left, strong ideological sympathies and a inclination towards domination the fiends should be all about the sword.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 06-29-2023, 12:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T07:58:13-07:00","id":1516650,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1515556#post1515556\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Given the 5th edition metaplot updates, it's only sensible for the Tzimisce to have been all but destroyed. The majority of its methuselahs, elders, and ancillae have either been Beckoned, destroyed (regardless of by whom), or fucked off to participate in the Gehenna crusade. The gross excesses of the revenant families are too much to hide in the face of the SI to say nothing of the cartoonish villainy of metamorphosist Tzimisce. The clan's dalliance with sectarian politics ended exactly where it always would: the original Anarchs' work destroying clan identity and culture for a shared, sectarian, identity in the Sabbat meant the clan would all but die with it.<br /> <br /> Who's left? Tzimisce that didn't chug the Sabbat Kool-aid, young enough to avoid the Beckoning, and subtle enough to avoid the Second Inquisition. So...not very bloody many. It only makes sense they'd be &quot;back to square one&quot;: isolated, local domains ruled by broods of sires and their childer.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be honest, Tzimisce as a clan would destroy Eastern Europe before they are done dirty like that.<br /> I am talking about mountains crumbling, Black Sea flooding the earth, forest overrunning cities and Transylvania itself purged from any undesirable people like that. Basically, Eastern Europe turning into the Middle East in a sense of how fucked things are. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27335-technoma","name":"Technoma","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"103"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T08:04:58-07:00","id":1516651,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1516643#post1516643\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> why wouldnt they drink the kool-aid? From a tzmisce perspective the sabbat was/is right about everything. If anything as the only cohesive clan left, strong ideological sympathoes and a inclination towards domination the fiends should be all about the sword.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Do they though? It seems a lot of tzimisce, such as Oradea League and the Tzimisce of india, who arent apart of the sabbat prefer to be left to their own devices/use the sabbat as tools to further their own ambitions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/pub/18.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","name":"Hello","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27313_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T08:56:48-07:00","id":1516657,"title":"","content":"The Tzimisce of Russia, the Nordic Tzimisce (under Gunnhild, the &quot;Mother of Kings&quot; and Fiend-witch) India, Africa, the Old Word... not really Sabaat and basically who left in Europe mostly Elders by now or Old Clan - Velya the Vivisectionist is the Cardinal of the Land Beyond the Forest, but himself back when he was called <i>the Flayer</i>\u200b was an Elder and the as the Child of Triglav not only Koldun master but of the 5th Generation. Vykos more or less left the Sabbat to prusue its own goals, but passed its first millenium; Lambach was an ancient even when he Embraced Dracula and busy being a &quot;good lad&quot; and knows the Truth... Everyone who is &quot;somebody&quot; in the Sabbat and a Tzimisce except maybe Sz\u00e9chenyi Jol\u00e1n, the Mother of Horrors are Sabbat only nominally and not even in America.<br /> The truth is, all the &quot;young&quot; - or not that young back then - Tzimsice who joined/ formed the Sabbat are Elders now not much different than the Elders of the Camarilla with the streak of fierce territorial habits and large domains... back from weher they came from<br /> <br /> The &quot;stereotipical&quot; Sabbat Tzimisce are mostly concentrated to central America - far away from the rest to cause trouble elsewhere but &quot;home&quot;<br /> But the rest of the Clan?<br /> No, not really; noname pack members, maybe, a few ancillae here and there, but thats all<br /> <br /> It was a common &quot;knowledge&quot; the Lasombra was the <i>heart of the Sabbat</i>, and hte Tzimisce was the <i>soul.</i>\u200b<br /> But it is far from the truth - wasn't really true before V5 and in V5?<br /> Well... <br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T09:48:20-07:00","id":1516664,"title":"","content":"As I recall European tzmisce were still genrally Sabbat and it terms of population the majority of the clan certainly was to some degree, the ones who wernt usually bordered on autarkis and were typically eldar do would be gone with the Beckoning. India is a bit of a question mark in v5 as is Africa, its also never been particularly clear what numbers where talking about in those areas. I'm also unsure where you got the idea vykos is disinterested in the Sabbat considering his interactions in the v5 book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 06-29-2023, 01:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T09:54:32-07:00","id":1516667,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hello</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1516651#post1516651\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Do they though? It seems a lot of tzimisce, such as Oradea League and the Tzimisce of india, who arent apart of the sabbat prefer to be left to their own devices/use the sabbat as tools to further their own ambitions.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> the oradea league was noted to be a very small group mostly consisting of maybe a dozen elders centered around oradea Which are in turn probably gone in the Beckoning. Indian vampires in general are a big question mark in v5 so who knows?<br /> <br /> Their are plenty of non sabbat tzmisce but id say the clan is majority sabbat in the context as say ventrue are majority carmarilla.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 06-29-2023, 01:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T11:44:52-07:00","id":1516677,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1516667#post1516667\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> the oradea league was noted to be a very small group mostly consisting of maybe a dozen elders centered around oradea Which are in turn probably gone in the Beckoning. Indian vampires in general are a big question mark in v5 so who knows?<br /> <br /> Their are plenty of non sabbat tzmisce but id say the clan is majority sabbat in the context as say ventrue are majority carmarilla.</div> </div> </div> </div>Makes sense, guesss the negotitations between the sabbat and the tzimisce in india to aid with their war is never going to be brought up again or explored.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/pub/18.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","name":"Hello","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27313_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T15:18:54-07:00","id":1516696,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hello</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1516677#post1516677\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Makes sense, guesss the negotitations between the sabbat and the tzimisce in india to aid with their war is never going to be brought up again or explored.</div> </div> </div> </div>India is a bit awkward for 5th ed as its traditionally ravnos territory with the kuei jin in contention and nobody has the stones to touch that. They could make it carmarilla territory but that undermines the narrative the Carmarilla is western elites and in retreat of v5.<br /> <br /> Shame really, theirs a lot of cool stuff you could do with it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T09:02:44-07:00","id":1516793,"title":"","content":"My view on the current Tzmisce is that not much has changed with them with the sudden loss of the Sabbat and they are still doing there own plots or creating new factions based off old traditions that there elders created and having limited interactions with the Camarilla and Anarch sects that are transactional in nature. At least that what we are doing with them in our game when one of the Old Clan shows up to chat with our crew.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T12:49:09-07:00","id":1516816,"title":"","content":"They're split between the Anarchs and Sabbat, with just a few in the Camarilla (including the prince of Tokyo). Because the Sabbat has subsumed clan identity into sect identity, the Sabbat loyalists won't have much of a culture of their own. That means traditional Tzimisce clan culture is mostly found among the Anarchs, which is a natural sect for them because of their typical unwillingness to recognize a law beyond themselves.<br /> <br /> And despite the Dracula stereotype, the clan has been around for untold millennia, and the idea that it's been restricted to eastern Europe is beyond absurd. They've existed in Asia and Africa for thousands of years, and across the Americas for hundreds. There is zero possibility of there ever having been a Tzimisce monoculture across all continents on earth.<br /> <br /> The Sabbat was a specifically western European phenomenon that migrated to the Americas in the early colonial period. Like the Camarilla, it was a response to the Inquisition (itself a phenomenon centered in Catholic western Europe), which created the Anarch Revolt (during which many western European Tzimisce cannibalized their elders), and as a result of which there weren't really any Tzimisce methuselahs in the Sabbat to begin with, and it's only in the late modern period that they could be said to have any elders (as the neonates of the medieval period finally aged into that category). And despite the Sabbat's successes early on, the sect has always been an uncontrollable clusterfuck, not to mention anathema to the values of actual Tzimisce elders, which it was formed in direct opposition to. With all that in mind, although the Sabbat does bear the influence of the Tzimisce Anarchs that co-founded it, it can't be said to represent the clan as a whole, being formed out of a defiant rebellion against the clan as such.<br /> <br /> Of course, as much as it has tended to forget it, western/central Europe isn't the whole world. Lots of Tzimisce would have only come to be aware of the Camarilla and Sabbat in the colonial period, in which case they'd have no particular reason to identify with the latter. Even less so if clan identity actually mattered to them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":352,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","user":"Black Flag","date":". 06-30-2023, 03:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-01T01:56:53-07:00","id":1516868,"title":"","content":"I don't really think that's true. Pretty much all writting on the subject prior to v5 made it clear the Sabbat had entrenched territories in Europe partculary around traditional tzmisce and lasombra territories. The big difference appears to be the European Sabbat were more entrenched, cautious and less aggressive but they're still genrally overtly Sabbat.<br /> <br /> The 'traditional' tzmisce clan structure is unlikely to do well in the Anarchs. Its/it was hyper elder centralised, controlling and outright abusive to the point were it imploded during the 1st revolt. You could argue some or the more moderate/spinless territories might tolerate the clans possessive attitude towards humans, territory, childer and alien moral codes but its not going to go down well when the local Boyar insists the other clans pay tribute and genrally act like the carmarilla but with the bad bits of the Sabbat.<br /> <br /> <br /> Edit- on reflection the tzmisce have the same problem as lasombra in v5 in that dont thematically mesh very well with the edition. Their attitude to humanity and vampirism don't really align with either mechanical or thematic content and the dissolution of the Sabbat as presented in previous editions leaves them somewhat at a loose end since they don't really fit in the Anarchs and they'd be largely redundant in the carmarilla.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 07-01-2023, 05:31 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-01T03:00:10-07:00","id":1516872,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1516868#post1516868\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't really think that's true. Pretty much all writting on the subject prior to v5 made it clear the Sabbat had entrenched territories in Europe partculary around traditional tzmisce and lasombra territories.</div> </div> </div> </div>The early editions were written by people with a very faint grasp of European geography and history, and who hammered the lore into shape to conform to initial stereotypes (when the Tzimisce were nothing but an antagonist clan based on Dracula and Necroscope). Sure, the Sabbat spread beyond its original boundaries, but its entire origin and aesthetic come out of the Catholic west around the time of the Reformation and ensuing witch-panic. And in those lands few elders would have survive the Anarch Revolt to begin with. It\u2019s got no real connection to the Orthodox/Muslim world they\u2019re usually associated with (the authors were also apparently unaware of the prominence of Islam in the region). And that\u2019s without getting into the problem of restricting the clan to \u201cEastern Europe,\u201d which doesn\u2019t work to begin with.<br /> <br /> Those modern Tzimisce obsessed with the authority of age and lineage will find the feudal Camarilla appealing, and those that don\u2019t jibe with it will find that Anarch identity mostly lets them do what they want. The modern Anarch movement isn\u2019t devoid of hierarchy; it tends to be about how much power and influence an individual can hold without institutional structures in place to enforce it from above. And yes, you can get overthrown, but that\u2019s how it was in the dark ages too. But it\u2019s not the dark ages, the clan culture isn\u2019t stuck in the dark ages, and there aren\u2019t that many real elders on the scene anyway. In fact, it\u2019s previous editions in which the clan made no sense, being described as archaic to the point of anachronism, and obsessed with medieval forms of authority, in a sect that was actively antagonistic to all of the above. <br /> <br /> These two clans fit this edition just fine, if you take them seriously as global phenomena and aren\u2019t bound by stereotypes laid down before the authors properly understood what that meant, and aren\u2019t stuck in the rut of their initial conception as the two antagonist clans invented for the Sabbat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":352,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","user":"Black Flag","date":". 07-01-2023, 06:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-01T03:32:56-07:00","id":1516873,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Flag</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1516872#post1516872\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The early editions were written by people with a very faint grasp of European geography and history, and who hammered the lore into shape to conform to initial stereotypes (when the Tzimisce were nothing but an antagonist clan based on Dracula and Necroscope). Sure, the Sabbat spread beyond its original boundaries, but its entire origin and aesthetic come out of the Catholic west around the time of the Reformation and ensuing witch-panic. And in those lands few elders would have survive the Anarch Revolt to begin with. It\u2019s got no real connection to the Orthodox/Muslim world they\u2019re usually associated with (the authors were also apparently unaware of the prominence of Islam in the region). And that\u2019s without getting into the problem of restricting the clan to \u201cEastern Europe,\u201d which doesn\u2019t work to begin with.<br /> <br /> Those modern Tzimisce obsessed with the authority of age and lineage will find the feudal Camarilla appealing, and those that don\u2019t jibe with it will find that Anarch identity mostly lets them do what they want. The modern Anarch movement isn\u2019t devoid of hierarchy; it tends to be about how much power and influence an individual can hold without institutional structures in place to enforce it from above. And yes, you can get overthrown, but that\u2019s how it was in the dark ages too. But it\u2019s not the dark ages, the clan culture isn\u2019t stuck in the dark ages, and there aren\u2019t that many real elders on the scene anyway. In fact, it\u2019s previous editions in which the clan made no sense, being described as archaic to the point of anachronism, and obsessed with medieval forms of authority, in a sect that was actively antagonistic to all of the above.<br /> <br /> These two clans fit this edition just fine, if you take them seriously as global phenomena and aren\u2019t bound by stereotypes laid down before the authors properly understood what that meant, and aren\u2019t stuck in the rut of their initial conception as the two antagonist clans invented for the Sabbat.</div> </div> </div> </div>That doesn't really change anything and v5's writting doesn't really counter that. Clan culture barely exists in v5 especially with the tzmisce were it boils down to around 4 pages and what can be interpreted from additional info. Without the previous edition work what you're left with a Ventrue with a pinch of dracula<br /> <br /> v5 is hyper westernised and draws pop culture depictions of vamparism and 10-20's western cultural zeigheists so isn't actually global at all. It also has both explicit statements on vampiric nature which contradict both Sabbat clans ideology and outlook. They'd fit <i>better </i>into the carmarilla but are largely redundant their. You can draw on just how lacking the definitive traits both sects can be written that you can wedge them in but by that stage you've stripped away so much that your left with a non entity interacting with a non entity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 07-01-2023, 08:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-01T15:04:35-07:00","id":1516919,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Flag</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1516872#post1516872\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> ...It\u2019s got no real connection to the Orthodox/Muslim world they\u2019re usually associated with (the authors were also apparently unaware of the prominence of Islam in the region)...</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, the authors would have been <i>quite</i> aware of the prominence of Islam in the Balkans. The game was originally published in '91, the second edition came out in '92, and the Bosnian War started in '92 and went until '95. Reports and footage of basically every atrocity imaginable were nightly news fodder, and all but unavoidable. &quot;Drawing the veil&quot; over ethno-religious conflict in the Balkans was entirely understandable, and justified, given that context.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-01T16:24:50-07:00","id":1516924,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1516919#post1516919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oh, the authors would have been <i>quite</i> aware of the prominence of Islam in the Balkans. The game was originally published in '91, the second edition came out in '92, and the Bosnian War started in '92 and went until '95. Reports and footage of basically every atrocity imaginable were nightly news fodder, and all but unavoidable. &quot;Drawing the veil&quot; over ethno-religious conflict in the Balkans was entirely understandable, and justified, given that context.</div> </div> </div> </div>You'd think, yeah, and in the lore the Tzimisce were highly involved in Constantinople, which ought to place them at the heart of the Ottoman Empire from the start, yet they were rather conspicuously absent from the material on the Ashirra and the region in general. There's nothing inherently European or Christian about the Tzimisce clan, for example, and the fact that the historical Dracula ultimately fought against the Ottomans shouldn't have anything to do with the clan as a whole.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-01T16:37:26-07:00","id":1516926,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1516873#post1516873\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Clan culture barely exists in v5 especially with the tzmisce were it boils down to around 4 pages and what can be interpreted from additional info. Without the previous edition work what you're left with a Ventrue with a pinch of dracula.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's about as much clan culture as you could expect from large global populations, which is to say, not a lot. Clan identity is a thing that matters to some, not to others, and actual clan organization beyond the local level is going to be difficult. Clans as monocultures never really made sense to begin with, so I have a hard time seeing its absence as a problem here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> v5 is hyper westernised and draws pop culture depictions of vamparism and 10-20's western cultural zeigheists so isn't actually global at all. </div> </div> </div>Have no idea what you're talking about here. My point is that the clans are global, meaning they have members across the globe. They're not limited to specific geographic regions and ethnic groups, and it wouldn't make sense for the main clans to be so. A localized bloodline maybe, but not one of the Big 13.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It also has both explicit statements on vampiric nature which contradict both Sabbat clans ideology and outlook. </div> </div> </div>Clans don't have ideology. Sects do. Anyone embraced by a member of a given clan is of that clan, regardless of who does or doesn't buy into an ideology (which you can't enforce on individuals in any case, even with a blood bond). The only thing clans have in common is a bit of the personality of the founder, which exists below the level of individual consciousness and occasionally exerts its influence over the individual's behavior. Beyond that, every vampire is free to adopt their sire's viewpoints, or to decide they're full of shit. And while members of the same clan might feel some loyalty to each other, as mortal extended families do, even that is no guarantee.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-01T17:24:20-07:00","id":1516930,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Flag</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1516924#post1516924\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's nothing inherently European or Christian about the Tzimisce clan, for example...</div> </div> </div> </div>There isn't; by and large, the clan as of the Dark Ages and Renaissance cleaved pagan, and that put them in direct conflict with <i>both</i> Islamic and Christian vampires, and their herds. At first it was the Ventrue and their allies, using HRE &quot;colonization&quot; into the Balkans, and conflict with Islam came later with the Ottoman wars.<br /> <br /> The Sabbat shit came later, and was largely the project of Lasombra in mockery of Catholicism.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...and the fact that the historical Dracula ultimately fought against the Ottomans shouldn't have anything to do with the clan as a whole. </div> </div> </div>The Banu Haqim participated in the Ottoman wars. The Tzimisce as a clan very much had a stake in it; <i>their</i> domains were among those targeted by the Banu Haqim and Ashirra.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-02T01:22:44-07:00","id":1516951,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Flag</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1516926#post1516926\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's about as much clan culture as you could expect from large global populations, which is to say, not a lot. Clan identity is a thing that matters to some, not to others, and actual clan organization beyond the local level is going to be difficult. Clans as monocultures never really made sense to begin with, so I have a hard time seeing its absence as a problem here.<br /> <br /> <br /> Have no idea what you're talking about here. My point is that the clans are global, meaning they have members across the globe. They're not limited to specific geographic regions and ethnic groups, and it wouldn't make sense for the main clans to be so. A localized bloodline maybe, but not one of the Big 13.<br /> <br /> <br /> Clans don't have ideology. Sects do. Anyone embraced by a member of a given clan is of that clan, regardless of who does or doesn't buy into an ideology (which you can't enforce on individuals in any case, even with a blood bond). The only thing clans have in common is a bit of the personality of the founder, which exists below the level of individual consciousness and occasionally exerts its influence over the individual's behavior. Beyond that, every vampire is free to adopt their sire's viewpoints, or to decide they're full of shit. And while members of the same clan might feel some loyalty to each other, as mortal extended families do, even that is no guarantee.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Thats because 5th ed strongly downplays clan as idenitity and see it more as a character class. This leaves a lot of speculative blanks where previous lore fills in, now we can theoretically ignore this and just homebrew but that makes the thread pointless im not arguing for clan monoculture I'm arguing for culture.<br /> <br /> I'm arguing they're not global, they're very much western. The writting seems to think they're global but when you look at them they're all pretty much all grounded in European ideas and cultural traits. <br /> <br /> Clans don't have ideolgy but they have strong cultural trends through a mixture of historical inertia and physical traits the clan will lean strongly into certain behaviours and norms. For example the brujah will genrally have strong anarch sympathies, not everyone but enough to see a trend to the point were the Brujah outright left the carmarilla. By contrast Sabbat ideology strikes a lot of chords with tzmisce culture in a way the anarchs and carmarilla don't.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-02T06:05:05-07:00","id":1516965,"title":"","content":"The thing with clans is that sires tend to select for certain traits they find appealing. In many ways, that does introduce bias into clans. Most fledglings are also mentored/indoctrinated for a period by their sires. Finally, clan weaknesses (and now Compulsions) reinforce certain kinds of behaviour which make clan an even stronger influence for many vampires in V5 than sect affiliation.<br /> <br /> Sect affiliation, meanwhile, can also, at least partly, be about happenstance. Most Camarilla Kindred, in any edition, are Camarilla by default -- they were Embraced in a Camarilla city by a Camarilla sire, and so that's all they know. Switching sects, unless you're in a city under siege, usually means leaving your city and finding a new one.<br /> <br /> V5 troubles the idea of &quot;sect as structure&quot;, meanwhile, by removing most of that structure anyway, or simply making it inaccessible. The Camarilla has retreated into a kind of Old Boys' Club that most PCs have to actively join (rather than it being the default), limiting access to it's structure, and the Sabbat basically has no structure at all now.<br /> <br /> So the only structure most PCs will get to experience is the Anarch one -- which is relatively fluid, because they don't really have structure in the same way the Camarilla does. It's a &quot;make it up as we go along or maybe copy the Camarilla if we run out of ideas&quot; kind of structure. I think the intent was actually to enable to PCs to be more instrumental in making their own city-level political structures, and that can reasonably include a combination of both clan and sect structures.<br /> <br /> So \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200bI don't buy the &quot;clans don't have structure&quot; argument or the idea that sects inherently provide more structure. They just provide different kinds of structure that can interact in different ways. Just look at the Tremere as a clan that's very structured, or the Hecata. Compared to the Sabbat, those clans have way more structure than some sects have in V5.<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200bWatch actual plays such as LA by Night and you'll see that the way V5 is often played (including by some of the people who created this edition) does involve clans providing some sort of structure -- often a lot of it. Reacting against a structure also requires a structure to react against.<br /> <br /> Yes, clan isn't a singular, unitary thing that overrides all else, but the original clans were one of the attempts to answer the early question &quot;what do vampires do?&quot; when Mark Rein-Hagen and friends were creating 1e. So right from the beginning of the game's publication, they were intended to provide some sort of structure -- including conflict within and between clans, a guide for how to roleplay (including giving you the chance to play against type), the possibility of gaining different kinds of Status, and so on.<br /> <br /> Moreover, clans and sects are two axes of structure and character flavour that interact to create interesting and nuanced roleplaying decisions for players. Leaning into the possibilities of both is a good thing.<br /> <br /> All that being said, I think it's a fair question to ask what the Tzimisce clan structure might be like in V5, and &quot;clans don't have structure&quot; just closes down discussion and opportunities, rather than potentially opening them up. Just because the books haven't focused on clan structure for the Tzimisce doesn't mean we can't consider what one might look like.<br /> <br /> I personally really appreciate shadeprowler's very thorough contributions here. I think that a more Dark Ages version of the clan is probably the best way to go now.<br /> <br /> Hospitality, rules, etiquette and territory would provide a really interesting framework for V5 Tzimisce characters. Conversely, so would that kind of rebelliousness that sparked the Anarch Revolt, as the Tzimisce were instrumental in that. I could see fledglings being raised by a sire who could have taken either route, and that would shape their views (including whether they reject or accept that view).<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> The &quot;status quo&quot; Tzimisce might double down on their territorialism. For them, pledging allegiance to a sect is a way to keep what power and domain they already have. It's a smart move. They might instruct their childer to behave in a similar fashion and could feasibly maintain an Oradea League-like alliance with other Tzimisce across the region to maintain that status quo. Shared needs (their clan bane) mean alliances make sense.<br /> <br /> The &quot;anarch&quot; Tzimisce (not necessarily capital-A &quot;Anarchs&quot;), might be more loosely structured, and keen to refuse bondage. But they still have a clan flaw and a Compulsion that encourage certain kinds of behaviour, so how do they square that circle?<br /> <br /> In essence, I think they would likely pretend to be rugged individualists, who are masters of their own fates (and domains) and respect their peers' own freedom (and domains), but the effect might look a little like the status quo -- just as less of an &quot;alliance&quot; and more of a &quot;begrudging recognition that you stay there and I'll stay here, and we both keep what's ours&quot;.<br /> <br /> An Anarch barony could feasibly become something like a Voividate, too, if a Tzimisce winds up in charge. That could be a cool thing to look at. And then you have the obvious conflict of that: In rebelling against clan, do you end up more like your clan as a result?<br /> <br /> Either group could potentially be very isolationist or very social, depending on the characters involved. What happens, though, when boundary disputes occur? If domain is really important to Tzimisce, then I can see that occurring more frequently than we might initially expect. So perhaps they need a framework to adjudicate that in a way that respects sect rules and the domain of others, without engaging in turf wars that draw the SI. That then suggests other structures that may emerge organically as a result.<br /> <br /> I think more discussion of this idea is definitely a good thing. It can be potentially quite fruitful, regardless of whether we're &quot;doing it wrong&quot; or &quot;missing the point&quot; of V5.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-02T10:24:24-07:00","id":1516980,"title":"","content":"Honestly one thing that always irked me about the Tzimisce was that prior to V20, that the main Clan was depicted as Sabbat only while the Lasombra had members in the Camarilla and Anarchs. Hell even the Assamites in Rev had Anarch members yet the Tzimisce Clan never had significant members among the Anarchs or the Cam? It just felt weird that a Clan like the Tzimisce would stay so utterly devoted to one Sect when other Clans that are more hierarchical have significant numbers in multiple Sects.<br /> <br /> Thankfully V20 changed that with Anarchs Unbound where they retconned it, where there were Tzimisce that never left the Movement from start and in turn hate the Sabbat Tzimisce more than anyone else, seeing them as traitors to the Anarch Movement as well as stealing and perverting the Vaulderie ritual.<br /> <br /> I take the V20 stance on the Clan, with the main divide between the Clan has always been between the Sabbat and the Anarchs. Prior to the events of V5, the Sabbat wing was larger due in part to their position within the Sabbat and higher number of Embraces they performed. In V5, the numbers have begun to swing towards the Anarchs, with some Sabbat Tzimisce defecting to the Anarchs after seeing the Sect really going off the rails as well as the disappearances and deaths from the Gehenna Crusade thinning out the Sabbat numbers. And in turn mirroring the Lasombra defections to the Camarilla.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-02T15:09:22-07:00","id":1517005,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AkatsukiLeader13</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1516980#post1516980\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly one thing that always irked me about the Tzimisce was that prior to V20, that the main Clan was depicted as Sabbat only while the Lasombra had members in the Camarilla and Anarchs. Hell even the Assamites in Rev had Anarch members yet the Tzimisce Clan never had significant members among the Anarchs or the Cam? It just felt weird that a Clan like the Tzimisce would stay so utterly devoted to one Sect when other Clans that are more hierarchical have significant numbers in multiple Sects.<br /> <br /> Thankfully V20 changed that with Anarchs Unbound where they retconned it, where there were Tzimisce that never left the Movement from start and in turn hate the Sabbat Tzimisce more than anyone else, seeing them as traitors to the Anarch Movement as well as stealing and perverting the Vaulderie ritual.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm pretty sure that was from Tzimisce Revised?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-02T17:16:17-07:00","id":1517014,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1517005#post1517005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm pretty sure that was from Tzimisce Revised?</div> </div> </div> </div>Nope. I checked both the Rev CB and Guide to the Anachs and between the two of them there's only a couple of off hand mentions of a few Tzimisce Anarchs. But not enough to warrant an entry in the Clan section of GotA as the Lasombra did.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-02T17:36:02-07:00","id":1517016,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AkatsukiLeader13</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1517014#post1517014\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Nope. I checked both the Rev CB and Guide to the Anachs and between the two of them there's only a couple of off hand mentions of a few Tzimisce Anarchs. But not enough to warrant an entry in the Clan section of GotA as the Lasombra did.</div> </div> </div> </div>But from what I understood, non Sabbat Tzimisce were mostly independant? The CB clearly states that there are quite a few independant Tzimsice. They don't seem to account for Anarchs before Anarchs 20, but I may have misunderstood the crux of your statement. On a reread, you're not really insisting that there are no non-Sabbat Tzimisce.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-02T19:01:56-07:00","id":1517023,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1517016#post1517016\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> But from what I understood, non Sabbat Tzimisce were mostly independant? The CB clearly states that there are quite a few independant Tzimsice. They don't seem to account for Anarchs before Anarchs 20, but I may have misunderstood the crux of your statement. On a reread, you're not really insisting that there are no non-Sabbat Tzimisce.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah I wasn't really counting the independent Tzimisce because they're either Old Clan or loners, both of whom largely stay away from the conflicts of the Sects and tend to be isolated in general.<br /> <br /> Like I've said, it never really made sense to me that one of the major Clans behind the First Anarch Revolt all decided as one to become part of the Sabbat, especially when the other major Sabbat Clan and another major player in the Revolt, had viable numbers in the Cam and Anarchs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-03T08:22:27-07:00","id":1517065,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AkatsukiLeader13</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1517023#post1517023\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yeah I wasn't really counting the independent Tzimisce because they're either Old Clan or loners, both of whom largely stay away from the conflicts of the Sects and tend to be isolated in general.<br /> <br /> Like I've said, it never really made sense to me that one of the major Clans behind the First Anarch Revolt all decided as one to become part of the Sabbat, especially when the other major Sabbat Clan and another major player in the Revolt, had viable numbers in the Cam and Anarchs.</div> </div> </div> </div>In lore setting wise post Sabbat the Anarchs are redundant since the sword effectively does most of what they'd want from Anarchs and some other stuff. Its the difference between a hard line left wing party vs the democrat party ( or the us socialist party if I'm feeling mean) if you're a socialist. The Sabbat itself is effectively a product of the tzmisce and lasombra being left in charge of the movement asking them to remain in the anarchs is like having burger instead of steak.<br /> <br /> V5 obviously puts them in a difficult situation but it wasnt really designed with them in mind. Its more for the 7 and the thinbloods. Hence no Sabbat.<br /> <br /> Out of game it was probably because the tzmisce wernt initially designed as pcs and implicitly in opposition to how vtm was &quot;supposed&quot; to be played. Their utter rejection of humanity on every level went way further than lasombra combined with their alien brutality didn't really feed into personal horror as interpreted regretful slow degeneration not &quot;I'm so utterly horrific that I merged with my son and daughter and this made perfect sense internally and was a positive event from my perspective&quot; or &quot;im dracula so while I have admirable traits it'd be a horrible mistake to consider me relatable&quot; Personal horror<br /> <br /> I do like tzmisce in the anarchs but tend to run them as ones unsuited for the clan. Typically that they cannot or will not adopt a path. Considering sloppy Sabbat embracing. The other idea I had was the anarch tzmisce are either the orthodox children of the Dracon or the Koldun witch priests due to theological tensions with the Catholic Sabbat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 07-03-2023, 11:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-03T15:01:23-07:00","id":1517107,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1517065#post1517065\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> In lore setting wise post Sabbat the Anarchs are redundant since the sword effectively does most of what they'd want from Anarchs and some other stuff. Its the difference between a hard line left wing party vs the democrat party ( or the us socialist party if I'm feeling mean) if you're a socialist. The Sabbat itself is effectively a product of the tzmisce and lasombra being left in charge of the movement asking them to remain in the anarchs is like having burger instead of steak.<br /> <br /> V5 obviously puts them in a difficult situation but it wasnt really designed with them in mind. Its more for the 7 and the thinbloods. Hence no Sabbat.<br /> <br /> Out of game it was probably because the tzmisce wernt initially designed as pcs and implicitly in opposition to how vtm was &quot;supposed&quot; to be played. Their utter rejection of humanity on every level went way further than lasombra combined with their alien brutality didn't really feed into personal horror as interpreted regretful slow degeneration not &quot;I'm so utterly horrific that I merged with my son and daughter and this made perfect sense internally and was a positive event from my perspective&quot; or &quot;im dracula so while I have admirable traits it'd be a horrible mistake to consider me relatable&quot; Personal horror<br /> <br /> I do like tzmisce in the anarchs but tend to run them as ones unsuited for the clan. Typically that they cannot or will not adopt a path. Considering sloppy Sabbat embracing. The other idea I had was the anarch tzmisce are either the orthodox children of the Dracon or the Koldun witch priests due to theological tensions with the Catholic Sabbat.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can't really speak for the early days of VtM as I came in with the Rev Ed and while I've read some of the early books, it wasn't really something I was a part of. So I'm coming into this from the lens of the Rev Ed and V20, not 1E and 2E. Indeed I tend to view 1E in particular as proto-VtM as it's clear that they had a lot of ideas but not the clearest direction of where they wanted to take the game.<br /> <br /> So my first real experience with the Sects was in Rev Ed and it's three Sect books which like I said earlier had full Clan write-ups for the Lasombra in the Cam and Anarchs. Hell, the Assamites also had write-ups in the Anarch and Cam books. Yet the Tzimisce were only avalible to the Sabbat or the independent Old Clan hiding in Eastern Europe and pretending that the world hadn't change. Though like I said, the CB and GotA did mention off hand that there were some Anarch Tzimisce. Just apparently not enough to warrant a write-up in the book like the Lasombra. Throw in how the First Anarch Revolt was talked, it again just seemed so utterly weird that the Tzimisce as pretty one (barring the Old Clan) decided to go create the Sabbat while other Clans with strong connections to the Revolt didn't and instead went in several directions.<br /> <br /> It also doesn't help that the Tzimisce have long been typecast as either Eastern European fossils or utter inhuman fiends. In fact, of the 13 Clans the only ones I've had a harder time being able to play as at the table than Tzimisce were the Giovanni and that's more because prior to V5 (and to a lesser extent V20) they went way too hard on the Giovanni Neutrality, making plenty of people think that you could only play Giovanni PCs in Giovanni games. I've been at tables where the majority believe that fossils and fiends are the <b><i>ONLY</i></b> way to play the Tzimisce.<br /> <br /> As for the Anarch Movement post-Thorns, in the Rev Ed onwards (at least as far as I recall) the Movement never actually died. Sure, it was forced under the Camarilla Umbrella but the Movement persisted and eventually bounced back before the rise of the Free State, having been inspired by and involved in a number of the major Revolutions that the world saw in the 1700s, 1800s and early 1900s. And like I said, in GotA the Lasombra had a full write-up, depicting them primarily as Lasombra Anarchs who didn't join the Sabbat or descended from them which what they would later do with the Tzimisce Anarchs in V20. Again, why if they were willing to do that for the Lasombra who were commonly depicted as sociopaths with a survival of the fittest mentality but not the Tzimisce? It never made sense to me. Why do the Lasombra get more options and depth but the Tzimisce don't?<br /> <br /> I could understand if the Tzimisce were a Bloodline or akin to the Baali, intended as an NPC Clan but weren't. At least not with the Editions of VtM I know. Like I said, it's one of those things that long irked me before V20.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-03T15:33:04-07:00","id":1517112,"title":"","content":"Maybe because the Tzimisce <i>are</i> intended as an NPC Clan, or at the very least, they seem like it.<br /> <br /> After all, when Count fucking <i>Dracula</i> is one of the more <i>normal</i> looking and inconspicuous of your family, you can hardly say they're meant to be player characters, can you?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3201,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3201-father-enoch","user":"Father Enoch","date":". 07-03-2023, 07:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3201-father-enoch","name":"Father Enoch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3201_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"285"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-03T17:04:36-07:00","id":1517122,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AkatsukiLeader13</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1517107#post1517107\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I can't really speak for the early days of VtM as I came in with the Rev Ed and while I've read some of the early books, it wasn't really something I was a part of. So I'm coming into this from the lens of the Rev Ed and V20, not 1E and 2E. Indeed I tend to view 1E in particular as proto-VtM as it's clear that they had a lot of ideas but not the clearest direction of where they wanted to take the game.<br /> <br /> So my first real experience with the Sects was in Rev Ed and it's three Sect books which like I said earlier had full Clan write-ups for the Lasombra in the Cam and Anarchs. Hell, the Assamites also had write-ups in the Anarch and Cam books. Yet the Tzimisce were only avalible to the Sabbat or the independent Old Clan hiding in Eastern Europe and pretending that the world hadn't change. Though like I said, the CB and GotA did mention off hand that there were some Anarch Tzimisce. Just apparently not enough to warrant a write-up in the boo<br /> k like the Lasombra. Throw in how the First Anarch Revolt was talked, it again just seemed so utterly weird that the Tzimisce as pretty one (barring the Old Clan) decided to go create the Sabbat while other Clans with strong connections to the Revolt didn't and instead went in several directions.<br /> <br /> It also doesn't help that the Tzimisce have long been typecast as either Eastern European fossils or utter inhuman fiends. In fact, of the 13 Clans the only ones I've had a harder time being able to play as at the table than Tzimisce were the Giovanni and that's more because prior to V5 (and to a lesser extent V20) they went way too hard on the Giovanni Neutrality, making plenty of people think that you could only play Giovanni PCs in Giovanni games. I've been at tables where the majority believe that fossils and fiends are the <b><i>ONLY</i></b> way to play the Tzimisce.<br /> <br /> As for the Anarch Movement post-Thorns, in the Rev Ed onwards (at least as far as I recall) the Movement never actually died. Sure, it was forced under the Camarilla Umbrella but the Movement persisted and eventually bounced back before the rise of the Free State, having been inspired by and involved in a number of the major Revolutions that the world saw in the 1700s, 1800s and early 1900s. And like I said, in GotA the Lasombra had a full write-up, depicting them primarily as Lasombra Anarchs who didn't join the Sabbat or descended from them which what they would later do with the Tzimisce Anarchs in V20. Again, why if they were willing to do that for the Lasombra who were commonly depicted as sociopaths with a survival of the fittest mentality but not the Tzimisce? It never made sense to me. Why do the Lasombra get more options and depth but the Tzimisce don't?<br /> <br /> I could understand if the Tzimisce were a Bloodline or akin to the Baali, intended as an NPC Clan but weren't. At least not with the Editions of VtM I know. Like I said, it's one of those things that long irked me before V20.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Well my first contact is dark age and revised.<br /> <br /> It makes perfect sense, the clans revolt had largely been succesful and overthrown the old order. The other clans outside the lasombra did not do that. The tzmisce committed to the Sabbat because of a personal and clan level it was the best option.<br /> <br /> Yes that's because they're initially an npc antagonist clan and even afterward the clan is still quite an aquired taste. Theirs no obligation to play or run them and conceptual dilution doesn't do them any favours. As a side I'm unclear why it's objectionable that the tzmisce are elderich, cruel and arcane lords of the dark forests. Its like complaining that nosferatu look like the stuff of nightmares or the brujah are passionate rebels.<br /> <br /> Mostly because lasombra are more relatable and 'human' . For all their posturing about being Machiavellian social dawinst sociopaths alot of the clan still have a lot of the needy damaged human in them, it's part of their hypocritical charm. By contrast the tzmisce are so inhuman that they're genrally very much in sync with the Sabbat ideals in a way which is alarming-they reject humanity on a level and scale were the majority of the clan in on paths in modern nights. Now I don't really have a problem with anarch tzmisce especially in v5 since the Sabbat may as well not exist but prior to this the other sects didn't really have much to offer the the fiends. From Their perspective the anarchs were redundant and the carmarilla repulsive and that's before we get into their attitude to humans or humanity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 07-03-2023, 08:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-03T18:34:02-07:00","id":1517125,"title":"","content":"Prior to 5E most Tzimisce were in the Sabbat. There were a handful in the Camarilla or the Anarchs, and then there were the Old Clan who were part of the True Black Hand. But most of them had little issue with the Sabbat, and indeed their monstrous nature typically made it difficult for them to function in the other Sects.<br /> <br /> In 5E I believe the Sabbat Tzimisce, similar to the Lasombra, would have had a major split with many of them choosing to leave the Sabbat and join the Anarchs. Many Tzimisce were on the Path of Metamorphosis, which simply does not function at all, whatsoever, with the new Pact-based system that the Sabbat has devolved into. Those Tzimsci would have had little choice but to either leave their Path and get on one that was more Pack friendly, or leave the Sabbat, and since the Camarila doesn't welcome vampire on Paths, by default they would end up joining the Anarchs. I imagine the Anarchs largely have a &quot;live and let&quot; policy, and as long as these Tzimisce kept their horrors to their own domains and weren't attracting attention, the Anarchs would be more than happy to have them among their number.<br /> <br /> Fortunately for the Brujah and the Ministry these Tzimisce would probably be more interested in their own personal studies and pursuit of Azi Dahka rather than in Sect politics, would tend to operate as allies kept at a slight distance rather than seeing the Tzimisce try to exert control over the Sect the way you're probably going to see the Lasombra do in the Camarilla.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-04T03:28:37-07:00","id":1517150,"title":"","content":"Trouble with metamorphosis in v5 is it doesnt work well with the mechanics. Having touchstones is almost certainly the a level 1 and 4 sin and contradicts the paths philiolsophy plus vissitude is an absolute necessity.<br /> <br /> The faction i can see getting involved with the anarchs would be the children of the Dracon since they deliberately set themselves in opposition to the main clan.<br /> <br /> the more I think on it my solution to v5 tzmisce (outside of ignoring v5 sabbat writting) would be to make them independent like the heceta. The collapse of the Sects on the larger scale would make it pretty easy for them to establish domains in abandoned Sabbat territories and they could enforce cohesion with the vaulderie and re-establish the voivoide, with Sabbat remnants under thumb. This would neatly circumvent the issues with the other sects while maintaining clan identity and allowing individual fiends to join coteries.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 07-04-2023, 08:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-07T06:41:42-07:00","id":1517347,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514702-v5-tzimisce-clan-structure?p=1517065#post1517065\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> In lore setting wise post Sabbat the Anarchs are redundant since the sword effectively does most of what they'd want from Anarchs and some other stuff.</div> </div> </div> </div>Absolutely not. The Sabbat is essentially fascist, preaching freedom while relying on brainwashing to replace individual identity with group identity and a militant us-vs.-them mentality. It's also lost the basic reason why the Anarchs rebelled in the first place and replaced it with a fanatical crusade against all elders for eschatological, rather than liberatory, reasons. And whereas most Anarchs live quite closely with mortals and may be some of the most humane vampires, the Sabbat are separatist supremacists who actively discourage identifying with mortals. The two sects might have a common origin, but the Sabbat represents that origin being perverted beyond recognition, as the means <i>became </i>the ends.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Its the difference between a hard line left wing party vs the democrat party (or the us socialist party if I'm feeling mean) if you're a socialist. The Sabbat itself is effectively a product of the tzmisce and lasombra being left in charge of the movement asking them to remain in the anarchs is like having burger instead of steak. </div> </div> </div>What? I can say, as an actual socialist, that not having any socialist representation in US politics is very much a problem. And no, the Democratic Party is not remotely socialist, except in the eyes of the far right, to whom anything left of Mussolini looks the same. By definition, you can't achieve socialist aims through an institution so utterly beholden to capitalism.<br /> \u200b<br /> Also, some of the most illustrious Tzimisce Anarchs like Ruthven never left the movement, according to lore going back to the 90s. They never wanted to join a brainwashing doomsday cult and, most likely, realized from the beginning that the Sabbat was the product of some Lasombra &amp; Tzimisce rebels wanting to create a cult that they could use as a weapon in the Jyhad, believing (incorrectly) that they would be able to control it. But over several civil wars, the consistent lesson has been that the loyalist <i>antitribu </i>have proved impossible to control and the sect's doctrines (originally constructed to further the aims of its founders) are now self-perpetuating, no longer a means of control but <i>in </i>control of adherents. Thus there's no room in the sect for anyone but the true fanatics who think they're doing Caine's bidding and that he'll return any day now and reward them for it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> V5 obviously puts them in a difficult situation but it wasnt really designed with them in mind. Its more for the 7 and the thinbloods. Hence no Sabbat. </div> </div> </div>All 14 clans are presented together as equal choices in all materials at this point, and it's been that way for a couple of years now. It's true it took longer to develop the clans outside the original 7, but a lot of that was the problem of reframing them so as not to require the Sabbat to have them make sense. If any clan can belong to any sect, then the Tzimisce can't be Clan Sabbat. Hence the number of drafts of the clan that got returned for revision before they got it right.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Out of game it was probably because the tzmisce wernt initially designed as pcs and implicitly in opposition to how vtm was &quot;supposed&quot; to be played. Their utter rejection of humanity on every level went way further than lasombra combined with their alien brutality didn't really feed into personal horror as interpreted... </div> </div> </div>Utter rejection of humanity is pretty much entirely a Sabbat thing. The Tzimisce in the lore from pre-modern times were not necessarily any less humane than any other clan. Again, the Sabbat's idea of metamorphosis is a perversion of the classic Tzimisce dream of guiding the evolution of both vampire and mortal kind in a novel and productive way (the iconic example being Constantinople). And while alternative philosophies did exist in the medieval period, the Sabbat's hardcore rejection of humanity was as much a reaction against the Convention of Thorns as anything. A great many features of the Sabbat are basically just them defining themselves in opposition to the nascent Camarilla, not because the original Anarch Revolt actually entailed or required any of them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-07T07:10:17-0